463:
No motor vehicle shall utilize any road or street within the City of Brooklet other than Highway 80 that exceeds the weight limitations prescribed by state law for county roads (presently, O.C.G.A. § 32-6-26(±) and (g)). At the time of the adoption of this article, a maximum total gross weight limit of a vehicle and load shall not exceed 56,000 pounds unless said vehicle is making a pickup or delivery on such road. Provided however, such weight limitations may be exceeded for any vehicle without first obtaining a state permit when the load on any single axle does not exceed 23,000 pounds, the load on any tandem axle does not exceed 46,000 pounds, and the maximum total gross weight limit of the vehicle and load does not exceed 80,000 pounds when:
(1) Hauling forest products from the forest where cut to the first point of marketing or processing;
(2) Hauling live poultry or cotton from a farm to a processing plant;
(3) Hauling feed from a feed mill to a farm;
(4) Hauling granite, either block or sawed, or any other naturally occurring raw ore or mineral for further processing, from the quarry or stockpile area to a processing plant located in the same or an adjoining county and construction aggregates hauled to any point, unless otherwise prohibited;
(5) Hauling solid waste or recovered materials from points of generation to a solid waste handling facility or other processing facility;
(6) Hauling concrete that is in a freshly mixed and unhardened state for delivery to a customer; or
(7) Hauling poultry waste from the point of origin to a farm.
No lift axle may be used in computing the maximum total gross weight authorized for any vehicle or load under this section.
(Ord. of 10-21-2021(1))

472:
(a) Upon approval of the mayor and council, the chief of police is authorized to designate and maintain appropriate traffic control signs, markings and devices as follows:
(1) Cross walks at intersections where there is particular danger to pedestrians crossing the roadway;
(2) Other safety zones for pedestrians;
(3) Traffic lanes;
(4) Speed, parking, truck or other traffic control zones;
(5) Stop, yield, one way and other directional devices;
(6) Designated parking spaces and no parking zones or areas; and
(7) Any other signs, markings, devices or zones necessary for orderly and safe conditions of the roads and streets of the city.
(b) All traffic control signs, signals, devices and markings shall conform to specifications in the Manual On Uniform Traffic Control Devices adopted by the Georgia Department of Transportation. All signs and signals required under this section for a particular purpose shall, as far as practicable, be uniform as to type and location throughout the city. All traffic control devices so erected and not inconsistent with provisions of state law or this Code shall be official traffic control devices of the city.
(c) The city clerk shall keep and maintain an accurate official map setting out all traffic zones, markings, signs and other traffic control devices. The map and any amendments or changes to such map shall be adopted by the mayor and council, and the map shall be known as the official traffic control map of the city. An official copy of such map shall be kept in the office of the city clerk and shall be available to the public; copies certified by the city clerk shall be admissible in court as proof of the location of any traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device.
(d) No person shall be charged with violating a traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device unless appropriate signs, markings or devices are in fact operating or in existence on the streets involved.
(e) Any violation of any traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device established in this section shall be a violation of this Code.
(Ord. of 10-20-2022(1))
